eport into the  canadian military's justice system  tabled in parliament today reveals  sexual misconduct is just as rampant and  destructive now  as it was in 2015. that's when a  landmark federal report first documented  the extent of the problem  mercedes stephenson on the  recommendations in today's report  and the timeline for implementing them  this is the most comprehensive  independent review of the military  justice system  since the 1990s in the six years that  justin trudeau's  self-styled feminist government has been  in power things have not improved for  victims of sexual misconduct in the  military  former supreme court justice morris  fish's scathing review of the military  justice system  comes in the midst of a sexual  misconduct firestorm for the forces  the third such controversy in 30 years  the process is  i think very broken victims of sexual  misconduct have been failed by  successive governments  and they say they have little to no  confidence in the military  police and the military justice system i  don't think that they have enough  resources and personnel  qualified at the level for this type of  investigation  the landmark report is over 400 pages  long  with 107 recommendations for a  significant overhaul  of the military justice system the most  striking recommendation  for the civilian justice system to step  in and take over  investigating and trying sexual offenses  from the military  until the forces introduced reforms and  protections for victims  that was supposed to happen two years  ago but has never been implemented  we have a broken military law system  fish did not  recommend removing sexual offenses from  the military justice system  as many victims rights groups and  experts have  i don't think it's gone as far as i  would like it to be  for the sake of those victims the  military's top judge  is promising a better future military  justice  actors have been very reluctant to  change as the current  judge advocate general i fully embrace  the  recommendations made by justice fish the  liberal government says they're  committed to accepting fish's  recommendations  in principle but would not provide  specifics on a timeline for the changes  a critical piece of the puzzle victims  say they continue to hope for change  but will believe it when they see it  mercedes stevenson global news  ottawa  next a massacre of hundreds of black  americans you likely  have never heard about  one hundred years ago on this day there  was horror going on  in tulsa oklahoma a white mob burned  down one of the wealthiest black  communities in the country destroying  homes and businesses  killing hundreds of people bodies were  buried in unmarked graves  it was a massacre driven by racism and  the story of what happened in tulsa has  largely been forgotten or  erased hell was unleashed  literal hell was unleashed through the  night  and in the morning the mob terrorized  green with torches and guns  shooting at will as soon as it happened  there was a clear effort to erase it  from our memory  today u.s president joe biden visited  tulsa as part of commemorations there  he is the first president to do so  jackson proskow reports on today's  events in a warning some images in his  story  are disturbing the burning of black wall  street lasted  two days in 1921 a white mob  torched the prosperous black community  of greenwood  murdering dozens of residents robbing  them of their lives and livelihoods  thousands of black tulsans were arrested  and held for weeks  i described the massacre as the worst  single  incident of racial violence in american  history for the past 45 years historian  scott ellsworth has worked to expose the  tulsa race massacre  an atrocity wiped from the history books  and rarely spoken about  even by survivors the question is why  haven't i heard of this before  and the reason was that for a long time  people made sure that you weren't  going to hear about it no one has ever  been held accountable  the death toll of 300 is still just an  estimate  i'm a survivor of the tulsa race  massacre  survivors like 107 year old viola  fletcher have begun to tell their  stories  as they plead for a formal reckoning i  hear the screams  i have lived through the massacre every  day  a country may forget this history but i  cannot  these charred pennies collected by a  five-year-old survivor named george  monroe  are a relic of the destruction the mob  brought and a reminder of the wealth  that was destroyed  100 years later greenwood is still a  shadow of what it once was  a new generation of entrepreneurs is  pushing for reparations  and restoration if the land  was deeded back to people that had  some tie i think that would be  the the easiest way to start to repair  the atrocities of the past the recent  discovery of mass graves  shows there's still so much to account  for dozens of suspected victims were  buried anonymously at  sites across tulsa these are murder  victims these are these are massacre  victims right here  it's unlikely that all will be  identified but they will for the first  time  be acknowledged as tulsa seeks to heal  and as americans learn about their own  hidden history  jackson prosco global news washington  and that is global national for this  tuesday i'm donna friesen  across canada today people stood silent  in tribute to the indigenous children  who are believed to have been buried in  unmarked graves  at the former kamloops indian  residential school in british columbia  and at 2 15 local time the bells of the  hillhurst united church in calgary rang  215 times  once for every child thanks for watching  take care of yourselves  bye
 

